The server's environment configuration surface.

Every documented flag needs a variable behind it, and a variable set wrong must
not look like one that is unset.

- --disable-file-uploads gets CS_DISABLE_FILE_UPLOADS. Upstream left the flag
  CLI-only and Composery passes no CLI flags, so the documented variable had
  nothing reading it at all: uploads stayed on while the operator believed they
  were blocked. The help text names it for the same reason every sibling flag
  does.
- Every one of these switches is read through envFlag(), the overlay module that
  is now the only definition of "set to 1 or true" in the product. Upstream's
  inline regex answered differently from the API's own reading of the same
  sentence, so CS_DISABLE_PROXY=TRUE left the proxy up while
  COMPOSERY_DISABLE_API=TRUE took the API down.
- An unparseable idle timeout throws instead of being logged and ignored, so a
  typo cannot silently disable the timeout.
- The app name is ours rather than an environment default.

The variables are written in upstream's spelling on purpose: rebrand.mjs owns
every rename, so CS_DISABLE_FILE_UPLOADS becomes COMPOSERY_DISABLE_FILE_UPLOADS
on the assembled tree and there is exactly one table to change. No patch in this
stack renames an upstream variable. Variables Composery introduces outright
(COMPOSERY_DISABLE_AUTH, COMPOSERY_CLOUD_BOX_ID) have no upstream spelling to
rename, so they are written as themselves where they are read.

